WEBVTT

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:09.930
For 500 years, these birds have been surrounded by myth and glamour.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:16.240
And I've got to confess that I've been fascinated by them for most of my life.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:26.920
This is just one member of a hugely varied family that, to my mind, includes the most spectacular and beautiful birds on Earth.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.640
The birds of paradise.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.200
And what's more, they throw light on some of the great mysteries of evolution.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:52.560
Why have the birds of paradise become the most diverse, bizarre and beautiful of all bird families?

00:00:54.040 --> 00:01:00.000
Why have they developed the most extravagant plumes and adornments of any group of living

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.790
things on Earth so that sometimes they almost cease to look like birds at all

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:13.250
and why is it that this extraordinary family is largely restricted to one

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:24.000
jungle covered island in the Pacific explorers and scientists have been

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
puzzling over these questions for 500 years even today by using the

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:39.610
latest filming techniques we're making new discoveries about their behavior this surely

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:59.840
is one of the most spectacular sights anyone could see in the natural world the mystery

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:06.820
of the Birds of Paradise began back in the 16th century.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:10.900
In 1522, a ship returning to Europe

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.920
from exploring the mysterious islands of the Far East

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.300
brought with it, amongst other marvels,

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.000
three extraordinary skins.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.240
They were very like this one.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.110
You can see it's a bird, there's its beak and its head,

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:29.700
and here are these long feathery plumes.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.650
but it has no wings, and no feet.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.920
The explorers have been told that that was because these birds lived in paradise.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:50.430
The ship concerned was one of five that had set out in 1519

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.950
to sail around the world for the very first time

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:58.550
under the command of the Portuguese explorer Ferdinand Magellan.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.690
they endured catastrophic tropical storms and shipwrecks.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:13.410
Magellan himself was killed in a tribal war in the Philippines.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.910
But after three gruelling years,

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:21.600
the Victoria, the sole surviving ship, arrived back in Spain.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.860
It was loaded with wonders and treasures,

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.000
including those first specimens of birds of paradise.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:41.140
Magellan had been presented with these skins by a king in the Spice Islands, the Moluccas

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:47.170
as we call them today in eastern Indonesia. When Magellan's men asked why they had no

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:52.470
wings or no feet, the people had a problem because they themselves had never seen the

00:03:52.470 --> 00:04:00.000
birds alive. They had been traded to the islands from islands even farther to the east. So they made up

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:06.540
They said, well, it's because the birds float high in the sky among the clouds, feeding on dew,

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:10.960
and human beings only see them when they die and fall to the earth.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:18.900
So the first descriptions of these birds of the gods were far from firsthand,

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:22.310
yet they were accepted as fact by Europeans.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.000
This was one of the very first paintings of a bird.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.570
of Paradise, and it appears in the margin of a Book of Prayers, written in 1540, to show

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:43.950
the devout the sort of creatures they might expect to see when they got to Paradise. But

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:50.590
it wasn't only the pious who were interested in the discovery, so were naturalists. But

00:04:50.590 --> 00:05:00.000
their understanding of the birds was similarly clouded by mythology. This is the first volume

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.350
in a great encyclopedia of natural history,

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:07.890
published in 1599 by an Italian called Aldermanus.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:12.730
And it's full of remarkably accurate pictures and descriptions.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.070
I mean, there's a toucan, for example.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.790
And here, the hornbill.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:26.550
But turn another couple of pages, and a bird of paradise.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:30.000
Without legs, floating in the sky,

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:40.800
no wings. And here it is, drinking dew from the clouds. Altavandus was so respected that

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:49.070
this view of the habits of birds of paradise persisted well into the 17th century. It's

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:54.510
hardly surprising that these pictures are wildly inaccurate, bearing in mind that they

00:05:54.510 --> 00:06:00.000
were drawn from those flattened skins. After all, no one in Europe

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.200
had ever seen wings or legs attached to these astonishing prunes.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:11.060
So it was not unreasonable for Europeans, who still believed in dragons and mermaids,

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:15.340
to accept that these birds lived in paradise.

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:20.560
But still no one knew where the skins actually came from.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:25.450
In fact, the birds come from New Guinea.

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:29.440
It's a thousand miles long and it lies just north of Australia.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:36.060
And there, of course, the people knew perfectly well the truth about the birds.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:40.540
They hunted them for the sake of the plumes, which they used as currency and in many of

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:44.360
their important ceremonials.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:50.060
My first opportunity to see these wonderful birds came when I went to New Guinea back in

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:51.060
1957.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:57.770
We saw a wide, fertile valley ringed with mountains.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:00.000
This was our destination, the valley of the

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:07.580
Waagi River. Within a few minutes of landing I saw coming towards me through

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:12.840
the tall grass a party of tribesmen wearing magnificent feather headdresses.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:21.910
We filmed a celebration called a sing-sing during which tribal people

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:27.470
wearing spectacular headdresses of Birds of Paradise plumes gather together to

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:29.280
dance and chant.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:39.100
and i took these photographs they displayed them during their dances showing how wealthy

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:44.700
each of the men were by having these enormous headdresses that's princess stephanie's black

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:51.660
tail feathers this king of saxony's feathers from the top of the head these are the red plumes of

00:07:51.660 --> 00:08:00.000
count rages bird of paradise and these the yellow ones of the lesser when they came to have marriages

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.840
a party going to collect a bride would have to take a gift

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.000
to the bride's parents of Birds of Paradise plumes

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.080
and they arranged them on these great banners.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:15.140
There's a front view of that with, oh, nearly two dozen sets

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:18.360
of Birds of Paradise plumes all around the side of the banner

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.320
and down in the middle there, gold-lipped pearl shells.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.000
For thousands of years, the plumes have been traded

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.070
from this part of New Guinea, right across Indonesia,

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:36.850
up into Southeast Asia, and beyond.

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:44.560
In Europe, 400 years ago,

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.960
many aristocratic families possessed cabinets of curiosities

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.520
in which they displayed their collections of natural wonders,

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:57.360
and specimens of birds of paradise were amongst the most precious.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:11.620
Their splendor even caught the eye of British royalty.

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:17.650
The young Scottish prince, who was going to become Charles I of England,

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:20.650
had his portrait painted with his hat,

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:23.640
furry hat, on the table beside him,

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.820
and in it, his most treasured possession,

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.830
the plumes of birds of paradise.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:37.000
Naturalists, seeking to curry favour with the aristocracy and get financial backing

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:42.900
for their expeditions, promised in return to name any new species they discovered after

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:47.550
their patrons, and indeed they did so.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:53.190
This is Queen Carola's Bird of Paradise, with little plumes on the top of his head.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:58.890
This one was named after an Italian Count, Count Raj's Bird of Paradise.

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:00.000
This one was named after Queen Carola.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.520
victoria and this one is prince rudolph's bird of paradise so it's more commonly known these days

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:13.200
it's a blue bird of paradise and here is princess stephanie's bird of paradise with a great long

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:21.420
glossy black plume not all were named after royalty

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:30.000
napoleon bonaparte's nephew fired with republican zeal named this one di felodi

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.660
raise publica the republican or people's bird of paradise but the popular version of the name

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:47.910
didn't catch on and these days we call it wilson's bird unlike the showy males the female birds of

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:55.300
paradise are drab and brown in color all look very similar so you can well believe that they're

00:10:55.300 --> 00:11:00.000
closely related it's just the males with their extravagant

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.690
decorations that make the individual species look so different, but even as

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:11.510
late as the 19th century no European had seen anything of these birds except

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:16.000
their dried skins and people wondered what the living birds must look like.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:23.650
Oral Fuller, a collector who owns specimens of 37 of the 39 known species of

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:28.960
Birds of Paradise, also paints them and understands the difficulties involved.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:37.390
The early painters of birds couldn't go and see these things in the wild and they couldn't

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:44.620
see them in captivity. So they were presented with something like this. A dried, flattened

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:49.070
skin that had been brought back from New Guinea. And this was all they had to go on to make

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:54.890
their painting. This is a black sickle bill bird of paradise. And the problem they had

00:11:54.890 --> 00:12:00.000
were things like this. What on earth are these? They look at first sight like wings.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.420
But they're not wings. The wings are down here.

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:06.670
They're just ornamental plumes, and there are more ornamental plumes down here.

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:09.830
So what did the bird do with these in life?

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:17.780
This is a mid-19th century artist's answer, and it's wildly inaccurate.

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:23.060
The sickle bill actually displays like this.

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:30.000
It takes him a little time to work up to his friend.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:46.770
display posture he lifts up those feathery tufts on his shoulders and holds

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:55.680
them around his head so that he hardly looks like a bird at all and he repeats

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:00.000
the performance on the same display post up to five times

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:11.600
every morning it wasn't until 300 years after europeans saw the first skins that anyone actually

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:20.110
saw a bird of paradise displaying in the wild and the person who did so was the british explorer

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:26.130
alfred russell wallace who along with darwin first proposed the theory of evolution by natural

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:30.000
selection alfred russell wallace

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:33.080
was a great naturalist and scientist,

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:35.180
but he was not a wealthy man.

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.820
He earned his living by going to the tropics

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:40.180
and collecting insects and birds

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:42.600
and sending them back for sale

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.710
to wealthy collectors and to museums.

00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:49.260
And he was obsessed with Birds of Paradise.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:52.920
In 1854, he set off for New Guinea,

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:56.540
and eventually he became the first European

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:59.350
ever to see Birds of Paradise display.

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:09.400
Here is his description of that site: "On one of these trees, a dozen or twenty full-plumaged

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:14.720
male birds assemble together, raise up their wings, stretch out their necks, and elevate

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:25.770
their exquisite plumes, keeping them in a continual vibration. At the time of excitement,

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:30.000
the wings are raised vertically over the back, the head is bent down and stretched

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.240
out and the long plumes are raised up and expanded till they form two

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:48.800
magnificent golden families." Wallace's description amazed the world and his book

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:53.980
"The Travels in the Malay Archipelago" went on to become one of the great

00:14:53.980 --> 00:15:00.000
best-selling travel books of the 19th century. I myself read it when I was about 9 or 10

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:06.170
and the frontispiece to the second volume fascinated me.

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:10.030
Here are the birds, in display,

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:21.750
and I yearned to go off and see such a sight for myself.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:25.250
It was on that first trip to New Guinea in 1957

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:27.970
for a television series called Zoo Quest

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:29.960
that I got my chance.

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:41.580
During the first month, we saw plenty of plumes of Birds of Paradise on headdresses, but none on the living birds.

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:47.900
At just one syncing, I estimated that there were 20,000 bird skins on display.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:54.770
It seemed to me unlikely that we were going to find many Birds of Paradise alive around here.

00:15:55.980 --> 00:16:00.000
So we decided to travel somewhere further afield.

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:06.190
where there were fewer people in order to find the living birds.

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:12.190
We went to the north to a valley that was then quite unexplored, an uncontrolled territory

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:16.890
as they called it at the time, where the people were really still living in the Stone Age,

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:24.570
making stone axes like this. And we had to cross rivers with locally made suspension

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:30.000
bridges like this one, or even had to wade our way across. And we had a hundred porter

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:37.600
carrying everything we needed: food, gifts, cakes of salt, that sort of thing. But eventually

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:43.780
we did find the birds.

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:49.170
The ballet was throbbing with calls of Count Raj's Paradise Birds. For as far as we knew,

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:53.390
no one had ever filmed the courtship dance of these birds of paradise in the wild. And

00:16:53.390 --> 00:17:00.000
this was to be our lucky day. We could see his gorgeous red plumes,

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:06.650
hanging from beneath his wings, the plumes which make him so coveted and so desirable

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:13.760
a prize for all the people hereabouts. And then suddenly, in a frenzy of excitement,

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:17.460
he threw his ruby plumes above his head, shrieking with excitement.

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:24.020
Our film, even if it was in black and white and rather fuzzy,

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:30.000
was the first record of a wild bird of paradise in display, and showed exactly

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:39.320
how he erected his plumes and this skin which I found in the Paris flea market

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:44.200
some years ago is of the bird that we filmed in black and white and here you

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:49.950
can see how wonderfully rich its plumage was. This is a trade skin just as the

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:56.720
people prepare it in New Guinea without any legs and without any wings. Both have

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:59.570
been removed to emphasize the glory of

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:10.250
these prunes. After 10 minutes, he executed a final flutter and flew to another branch.

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:21.200
But this was only a single bird in display. It was another 40 years before I saw the group

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:26.990
display of the larger and more impressive species, the greater bird of paradise, that

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:28.640
Wallace had described.

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:33.650
the birds are in another

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:35.950
emergent tree just like this one

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:36.610
and I've got

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:39.510
an absolutely clear view of them

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:43.420
this at last is Wallace's picture

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:44.240
come to life

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:55.570
Wallace described the display very accurately

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:56.950
as you would expect

00:18:56.950 --> 00:19:00.000
but he didn't understand why the birds

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:13.370
were behaving like this, in a group. So even 300 years after the discovery of these birds,

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:22.120
the purpose of their displays still wasn't properly understood. And it wasn't just the

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:30.000
greater bird of paradise, the perplexed naturalists. The second species,

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.530
of bird of paradise to arrive in europe at the end of the 16th century

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:38.070
appeared to be an even more bizarre looking creature

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:42.430
it still had a pair of golden plumes sprouting from its flanks

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:46.720
to justify it being called a bird of paradise

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:49.840
it seems to have been painted soon after its arrival

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:54.160
as the gold color fades with time and like the first ones

00:19:54.160 --> 00:20:00.000
it had no wings or legs but it did have some extra rather mysterious

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:11.420
adornments. This is it. It's called the Twelve Wired Bird of Paradise. That's because it

